<h2>What low and slow means at Webstar</h2>
<p>Like a roast or a smoked brisket, the things that build trust cannot be rushed. <strong>Low and slow</strong> is how we talk about search and AI visibility after launch — not how we drag out your first website.</p>
<p>Some work should be fast. Initial builds and go-lives are: Simple sites often ship in about a day after complete intake; Business tiers in a few days; DNS cutover and form tests happen on a real checklist, not “eventually.” Deployments are deliberate but quick because scope is fixed in writing up front.</p>
<p>What does <em>not</em> hurry is reputation: human-written pages, review patterns, photos, and business profiles that stay accurate quarter after quarter. Organic discovery — classic search and AI-assisted answers — rewards that steady cultivation. We build the kitchen; you keep feeding it honestly.</p>

<h2>What is GEO?</h2>
<p><strong>GEO</strong> is marketing shorthand for “generative engine optimization” — a fancy label for <strong>being findable when AI tools summarize the web</strong>. The same idea shows up as AEO (answer engine optimization) or LLMO (large language model optimization). Different acronyms, same pitch: pay us to “win AI search” overnight.</p>
<p>Most of that is repackaged SEO from a decade ago — thin city pages, keyword stuffing, bulk blog posts — with new vocabulary. It is meant to confuse you and sell bulky packages. At Webstar, <strong>low and slow GEO is not a product line</strong>; it is the same discipline as solid local SEO, with extra emphasis on consistent facts across your site and listings so grounding tools can cite you without guessing.</p>
<p>For where AI actually pulls local facts from, see the companion guide: <a href="<?php echo webstar_h(webstar_url('resources/businesses-in-chatgpt-local-ai-search/')); ?>">Businesses in ChatGPT: Local Business AI Search</a>.</p>

