<h2>Ingredients: foundation every recipe needs</h2>
<p>Think of these as the base rub — skip one and the rest does not stick.</p>

<h3>One URL per intent</h3>
<p>Each page should answer one clear job: “furnace repair Macomb,” “half-day charter rates,” “book a color appointment.” Stacking five near-duplicate city pages or FAQ spam for the same head term dilutes trust and triggers scaled-content problems. When you expand, add <strong>real</strong> scope (a service, a case, a policy) — not another template with the city name swapped.</p>

<h3>Crawlable site</h3>
<p>Search and AI retrievers need plain HTML they can fetch: working internal links, a contact path that does not break on mobile, HTTPS, and sitemaps you submit in Search Console. Heavy builder chrome, broken menus, or forms that silently fail make you invisible and unusable — same outcome for humans and bots. Use the <a href="<?php echo webstar_h(webstar_url('resources/website-launch-checklist-domain-hosting/')); ?>">launch checklist</a> once, then spot-check after major edits.</p>

<h3>Honest NAP</h3>
<p><strong>Name, address, phone</strong> — and the URL you want cited — must match on your site footer, Google Business Profile, and any directory you care about (Bing Places, Yelp). VoIP numbers, old Wix URLs, or “stealth” suite addresses you do not staff erode citations in AI summaries and Maps. Fix mismatches the week you notice them, not “before the next SEO campaign.”</p>

<h3>Profiles worth maintaining</h3>
<p>A claimed, verified GBP with real categories, services, and photos beats a perfect homepage with an empty listing. Same for profiles you actually use — do not claim ChatGPT business beta or Yelp if nobody will update them. Step-by-step: <a href="<?php echo webstar_h(webstar_url('resources/google-business-profile-checklist-metro-detroit/')); ?>">GBP checklist</a>.</p>

<h3>Reviews with context</h3>
<p>Steady, specific reviews beat bursts of generic five-stars. “Fixed our heat pump in Shelby Township same day” helps matching more than “Great job!” Ask after wins; respond to every new one. Never buy bundles or scripted praise.</p>

<h3>Human content, lightly seasoned</h3>
<p>One edited case study, service expansion, or seasonal update per month is enough for most local shops. AI-drafted walls of “SEO articles” without your voice are empty calories — they crowd out work that compounds. Google’s people-first guidance applies whether the tab says SEO or GEO.</p>

<h2>Recipe card: weekly and monthly</h2>
<p>Full checklist detail lives in the <a href="<?php echo webstar_h(webstar_url('resources/businesses-in-chatgpt-local-ai-search/')); ?>">AI search companion</a>; this is the short version on the fridge.</p>
<p><strong>Weekly</strong> — review asks, review replies, NAP spot-check, mobile form/call test.</p>
<p><strong>Monthly</strong> — two real GBP photos, hours/services sync, one human page or refresh, quick Search Console glance, listing parity (Bing/Yelp if used).</p>
<p><strong>Quarterly</strong> — read homepage and services against how you actually sell; drop or update anything obsolete.</p>

<h2>What not to throw in the pot</h2>
<ul>
<li>Guaranteed “#1 AI” or instant traffic packages.</li>
<li>Dozens of AI blog posts with no trade-specific detail.</li>
<li>Keyword-stuffed city clones or hidden FAQ blocks meant only for robots.</li>
<li>Paid review farms, review gating, or fake profiles.</li>
</ul>
<p>Shortcuts spoil the batch. Platforms catch up; you are left with thin pages and a listing that does not match reality.</p>

